| 2026-10-22, same session | User asked explicitly whether `dbt_utils` (needed for `generate_surrogate_key()`) was actually investigated rather than assumed unavailable. Re-checked: found a governed `DBT_PACKAGES_EXT_ACCESS` external access integration already in the account with `USAGE` already granted to `SYSADMIN`. Investigated further and confirmed it only attaches to formally deployed `DBT PROJECT` objects via `EXECUTE DBT PROJECT`, not the interactive bash execution this build uses — adopting it would mean switching execution models for one hash function. Decision confirmed: local macro (**SD-17**) |
| 2026-10-22, same session | Created and pushed a `gold-layer` branch (**SD-16**) — first use of the new branch-per-step convention, confirmed landed in GitHub before building on it |
| 2026-10-22, same session | Confirmed read access to two `PRD_DW.DATAMART` objects before asking, then got explicit confirmation to reuse them read-only (**SD-18**): `v_cretex_calendar_date_dim` (`dim_date`) and `T_DIMPART_ALLERPS` (`dim_part`, filtered to our 3 companies) |
| 2026-10-22, same session | Built Gold: `dim_date`, `dim_part`, `dim_employee`, `dim_department`, `fct_labor` as dbt `dynamic_table` models. Hit two real errors, both fixed, not glossed over: (1) used production's *view* column aliases instead of the raw `T_DIMPART_ALLERPS` table's actual column names — caught by the compile error, fixed by checking `INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS` directly; (2) a Dynamic Table lag-chain violation (`fct_labor` at 1-hour lag depending on `dim_part` at 1-day lag) — fixed by aligning the reference dimensions to the same 1-hour cadence as the rest of the pipeline. 5/5 models built, 11/11 tests passed (including a hand-written grain-uniqueness test, since `dbt_utils.unique_combination_of_columns` isn't available per SD-17) |
| 2026-10-22, same session | Spot-checked `fct_labor`: totals match Silver exactly (33,460 / 325,025.2 / 13,085 earned hours for CDT/QTS/CCR — no loss in aggregation). New lead surfaced: **part-dimension match rate** is weaker than expected — CTX11002 27% of operations unmatched to `dim_part`, CTX11003 12.6%, CTX11101 negligible (0.1%). Flagged for the formal reconciliation step, not investigated further yet |
| 2026-10-22, same session | `gold-layer` → `main` via [PR #1](https://github.com/KrisK-ctx/ShadowCTXEarnedHours/pull/1), merged — first real use of the SD-16 branch-per-step convention, end to end. Verified independently from both sides: GitHub's `main` branch view and a direct `ls` from this Snowflake-side workspace both confirm the same files landed |
| 2026-10-22, same session | Created `reconciliation` branch. Grounded the comparison grain against live data before building: `LOT` is always NULL for all 108,966 Kinetic rows in production's target, so the real grain is company+job+operation_seqnum, not +assembly; checked that the 54 job-operations with multiple assemblies are all `MRP-D%` placeholder jobs with zero real labor, so no practical divergence |
| 2026-10-22, same session | Built `rec_labor_job_operation` (view, `PUBLIC` schema — explicitly a transitional/diagnostic artifact, not a permanent medallion layer, since it will be retired once a decision is made on this build vs. production). Full outer join of `fct_labor` against `CTX_EARNED_HOURS_PRD.GOLD.EARNED_HOURS_JOB_OPERATION` at matching grain, classified `MATCH`/`MISMATCH`/`ONLY_IN_SHADOW`/`ONLY_IN_PROD` |
| 2026-10-22, same session | **Root-caused the CDT -17.69% gap, found via row-level drill-down on 3 independent examples, all exact matches both directions**: production computes `earned_hours` from `JobOper.QUANTITY_COMPLETED` (Epicor's own operation-level running total), not from summing `LaborDtl.LaborQty` across labor transactions as our build did. Confirmed the mechanism: the current `MATILLION_RAW` extract doesn't carry full labor history, and reversal transactions introduce sign-flip noise — `QUANTITY_COMPLETED` is immune to both. **Fixed (SD-19)**: moved `earned_hours` to `int_labor_standard_hours.operation_earned_hours`, sourced by `fct_labor` directly instead of summing `int_labor_detail`. Rebuilt Silver→Gold→reconciliation (needed `--full-refresh` again, same dynamic-table change-detection quirk as before). **Result: CDT's gap closed from -17.69% to ~-3.5%** |
| 2026-10-22, same session | **Diagnosed the residual CDT gap (SD-20)**: the remaining ~484 job-operations are completely absent from the current `MATILLION_RAW.ERP_LABORDTL` extract (confirmed directly, e.g. job `SOJ201730` returns zero rows) despite production's fact showing real activity — a genuine source-data completeness limitation, not a modeling bug, and not fixed, since there's no SQL fix for data that isn't in the source being read |
| 2026-10-22, same session | **Root-caused the part-dimension match-rate gap (SD-21)**: sampled unmatched parts directly rather than guessing. Found two distinct causes — ~10% of CDT jobs reference angle-bracket placeholder/non-catalog parts (e.g. `<300701>`) never meant to match a real part master, and ~1% are genuine revision drift (job references part revision `A`, the DW's dimension has since moved to `B`/`B REDLINE`). Neither fixed — a structural limitation of reusing the DW's current-state-only part dimension (SD-18), documented as such. A residual beyond these two categories is not yet isolated |
| 2026-10-23, same effort, new session | User directly challenged the SD-20 "source completeness" claim: *"we are working with the same source data, so 'missing transactions' is suspect... use empirical evidence."* Traced production's own 46 `LABOR_SEQNUM` values for the test job by the true primary key (`LABORDTLSEQ`, not `jobnum`) and found all 46 rows **do** exist in the current raw extract — directly disproving SD-20. Found the actual mechanism: 20 of those 46 rows are stored with lowercase `jobnum` (`'bpq45915'`) in the exact same table that also has `'BPQ45915'`; a case-sensitive filter/join silently drops the lowercase rows. Case-insensitive summing reproduced `QUANTITY_COMPLETED` exactly. **SD-20 retracted** |
| 2026-10-23, same session | Quantified the case-variance problem empirically across all 3 companies before fixing anything: counted case-variant keys per column (`ERP_LABORDTL.jobnum` 244, `ERP_JOBHEAD.revisionnum` 7, `ERP_JOBHEAD.partnum` 3, `ERP_LABORDTL.resourcegrpid` 3, `ERP_EMPBASIC.empid` 1) and validated at full scale that case-insensitive transaction-summed quantity matches `QUANTITY_COMPLETED` for 99.32% of 109,830 operations — confirming the fix's scope before writing any SQL |
| 2026-10-23, same session | Created `case-normalization-fix` branch (SD-16). Added `upper(trim())` normalization to every affected Bronze natural-key column (`stg_ctxepicorkinetic__labor_detail`, `__job_operation`, `__job_header`, `__employee`, `__department`), plus the `dim_part` join condition in `fct_labor.sql`. Full-refresh rebuilt Bronze→Silver→Gold→reconciliation; all 28 tests passed/warned as expected (**SD-22**) |
| 2026-10-23, same session | Before deciding whether to revert `earned_hours` to transaction grain (now that the case fix makes it agree with `QUANTITY_COMPLETED` for 99.32% of operations), tested the residual 0.68% at scale: found it carries a disproportionate -2.97% of total quantity, concentrated in round-number gaps (900,000/600,000/275,000/... units) on a handful of very-high-volume CDT jobs — consistent with bulk/backflush completions never captured as individual labor transactions. Decided **not** to revert the grain — `QUANTITY_COMPLETED` stays authoritative (**SD-23**) |
| 2026-10-23, same session | Investigated a second pattern surfaced during reconciliation drill-down: operations with `production_standard_raw = 0` sometimes get a flat `est_setup_hours` earned-hours credit in production instead of an unresolved/null value. Implemented and tested this as a blanket rule — it improved CCR and was neutral for QTS, but made CDT's aggregate gap *worse* (-1.49% → +4.17%). **Reverted** rather than keep a rule proven wrong for one of the three companies; documented as an open, unresolved finding rather than silently applied (**SD-24**) |
| 2026-10-23, same session | Re-ran the full reconciliation suite after the case fix (SD-22) and the grain decision (SD-23): CDT's aggregate gap improved from -17.69% (pre-SD-19) to **-1.49%**; QTS **-0.67%**; CCR **-2.09%**. The part-match-rate gap (SD-21) dropped from ~11% unmatched to **0.36%** unmatched — case variance on `part_num`/`part_revision` was confirmed as the dominant driver of that gap, not placeholder parts or revision drift alone as originally characterized. Corrected the decision log (retracted SD-20, revised SD-19, added SD-22/SD-23/SD-24) rather than leaving the earlier, disproven framing in place |
| 2026-10-23, same session | Isolated the real SD-24 condition empirically (not the blanket rule): among zero-standard operations with `est_setup_hours > 0`, 88% of CDT's credited cases had `act_setup_hours > 0` vs. only 7% of non-credited ones. Implemented as `case when act_setup_hours > 0 then est_setup_hours else 0 end`, validated cross-company (CDT 96.18%, QTS 100.00%, CCR 98.52% match on the full zero-standard population). Row-match rate improved (CDT 74.19%→78.62%, CCR 60.04%→71.88%). This surfaced a new, separate, still-open ~5,700-hour CDT discrepancy that had been masking against the zero-standard gap (SD-25, logged at the time) |
| 2026-10-23, same session | User pushed back directly on the whole approach: *"I feel you are still missing something... If you were doing a thorough job of requirements gathering, we should be matching perfectly."* Stopped reverse-engineering from reconciliation data and read production's own Silver column-level documentation (`CTX_EARNED_HOURS_PRD.SILVER.FACT_LABOR`) directly instead. Found the real rule: Kinetic's per-unit rate is `est_production_hours/run_quantity` (not the 9-way `STANDARD_FORMAT` conversion built in SD-14 — only 87.67% agreement between the two, confirmed empirically), and Setup earns a fixed amount per operation based on `LABOR_TYPE_CODE` (R/Run vs. S/Setup), not on whether `production_standard_raw = 0` as SD-24 assumed |
| 2026-10-23, same session | User supplied one more piece of documentation (a Cretex Medallion PoC Business Design Document search) confirming Setup must be credited exactly ONCE per operation, using the final/current `SETUPPCTCOMPLETE`, with a documented historical defect (job 200013 op 21: a 5-hour setup estimate was being accumulated across 13 labor entries before being caught and fixed) as the precedent this build was unknowingly about to repeat |
| 2026-10-23, same session | Validated the corrected formula empirically before writing any SQL: `sum(laborqty * est_production_hours/run_quantity)` for Production + `est_setup_hours * (1.0 if complete else setuppctcomplete/100)` for Setup, credited once per operation. Ad hoc result against the full 3-company population: CDT 95.23%, QTS 100.00%, CCR 96.89% match — an order of magnitude better than anything achieved by the SD-13/14/19/23/24 iteration |
| 2026-10-23, same session | **Rebuilt the foundation (SD-26, supersedes SD-13/14/19/23/24, resolves SD-25).** Rewrote `int_labor_standard_hours.sql` (simple rate formula, operation-level Setup credit), `int_labor_detail.sql` (Production per-transaction, Setup carried separately to avoid double-counting), and `fct_labor.sql` (sums Production, `MAX()`s Setup — never `SUM()`s it). Added `assert_setup_credited_once_per_operation.sql`, a dbt test guarding against the exact historical double-counting defect. First full-refresh rebuild through the real pipeline scored lower than the ad hoc validation (CDT 83.86% vs. 95.23%) — investigated rather than accepted, and found two real implementation bugs: (1) the Setup completion override used `is_operation_complete OR is_job_complete`, but empirical counter-examples showed only `is_operation_complete` is correct; (2) `labor_type_pseudo` is blank on 214 real transactions (202 Production + 12 Setup, 459,660 units) where raw `labor_type_code` is correctly populated — switched the filter to the raw field; (3) `fct_labor`'s `MAX(setup_earned_hours_credit)` was being computed across ALL labor rows on an operation regardless of type, so operations with ONLY Production transactions could still pick up a Setup credit if `est_setup_hours` happened to be populated — fixed by only exposing the credit on Setup-type rows in `int_labor_detail`. After all three fixes: **CDT 96.66% row-match (-0.72% aggregate), QTS 99.80% (-0.0003%), CCR 95.45% (+0.19%)** — verified through the actual dbt pipeline, all 29 tests pass/warn as expected |

**On elapsed time:** from first reading the production architecture to having a governed, cost-capped, schema-structured sandbox live in Snowflake — including discovering and working around a GitHub-integration scoping issue and a session-mount bug — was same-day, with no infrastructure ticket, no manual DBA provisioning request, and no wait time beyond the two issues above. Bronze, Silver, Gold, and the formal reconciliation suite were scaffolded and built in the same working session; the earned-hours formula itself went through five iterations (SD-13/14/19/23/24) before being correctly rebuilt from the actual documented business rule (SD-26) rather than continued reverse-engineering, closing the gap from -17.69% to under 1% across all three companies. Semantic has not started yet; this timeline will be extended as it lands.

---

## 6. Current state (verified live against Snowflake, not assumed)

**Exists:**
- Database `SANDBOX_EARNEDHOURS_SHADOW` — schemas `BRONZE`, `SILVER`, `GOLD`, `SEMANTIC`, `PUBLIC`
- Resource monitor `RM_EARNEDHOURS_SHADOW` (50 credits/month, 0 used)
- API integration `KRISK_GITHUB_API_INTEGRATION`
- Git-backed workspace `USER$.PUBLIC."ShadowCTXEarnedHours"` ↔ `github.com/KrisK-ctx/ShadowCTXEarnedHours`
- dbt project `ctx_earned_hours_shadow` (`dbt_project.yml`, `profiles.yml`, `macros/generate_schema_name.sql`, `models/staging/`)
- **Bronze layer, live and tested, in `SANDBOX_EARNEDHOURS_SHADOW.BRONZE`:**
  - `stg_ctxepicorkinetic__company_enrollment` (view) — 3 rows (QTS/CCR/CDT, all active)
  - `stg_ctxepicorkinetic__labor_detail` (incremental/merge) — 221,394 rows across the 3 companies
  - `stg_ctxepicorkinetic__job_operation` (incremental/merge) — 213,818 rows
  - `stg_ctxepicorkinetic__job_header` (incremental/merge) — 19,094 rows
  - `stg_ctxepicorkinetic__employee` (incremental/merge) — 1,004 rows
  - `stg_ctxepicorkinetic__department` (incremental/merge) — 59 rows
  - All natural-key join columns normalized with `upper(trim())` (SD-22): `job_num`, `part_num`/`part_revision`, `employee_num`/`emp_id`, `resource_group_key`, `operation_code`, `jc_dept`
  - 12/12 dbt tests passing (`not_null` on business keys, `unique` on the enrollment gate)
  - `CHANGE_TRACKING=TRUE` enabled on all 5 incremental tables; `quantity_per_cycle` added to `job_operation`
- **Silver/intermediate layer, live and tested, in `SANDBOX_EARNEDHOURS_SHADOW.SILVER`:**
  - `int_labor_standard_hours` (dynamic_table) — Production per-unit rate (`est_production_hours/run_quantity`) and operation-level Setup credit (`est_setup_hours * completion-adjusted percent`), per the corrected business rule (SD-26, replaces the SD-14 9-way `STANDARD_FORMAT` conversion)
  - `int_labor_detail` (dynamic_table) — recomputed labor fact; `earned_hours` covers both Production (per-transaction, quantity-driven) and Setup (operation-level credit, carried but not summed here) direct labor (SD-26, replaces SD-13's Production-only scope), `has_unresolved_standard` flag (SD-15), native `EARNEDHRS` cross-check carried through
  - 27/27 hard tests passing, 2/2 `warn`-severity monitors firing as expected — both investigated and confirmed legitimate, not defects
  - **Early sanity check vs. production (Section 5):** superseded by Section 6's reconciliation suite below — kept here for historical trail only
- **Gold/marts layer, live and tested, in `SANDBOX_EARNEDHOURS_SHADOW.GOLD`:**
  - `dim_date` — DW calendar reused read-only (SD-18), key = `date_key`
  - `dim_part` — DW part dim reused read-only (SD-18), filtered to our 3 companies, key = `part_dim_key`
  - `dim_employee`, `dim_department` — conformed from Bronze, local surrogate keys (SD-17)
  - `fct_labor` — aggregated to company+job+assembly_seqnum+operation_seqnum; `earned_hours` = `SUM()` of the Production component + `MAX()` (never `SUM()`) of the Setup component (SD-26), with an explicit dbt test (`assert_setup_credited_once_per_operation`) guarding the double-counting defect this corrects for; `dim_part` join case-normalized (SD-22)
  - 12/12 tests passing, including a hand-written grain-uniqueness test and the new Setup-once-per-operation test (no `dbt_utils` per SD-17)
- **Reconciliation (diagnostic, transitional — not a permanent layer), in `SANDBOX_EARNEDHOURS_SHADOW.PUBLIC`:**
  - `rec_labor_job_operation` (view) — row-for-row comparison against `CTX_EARNED_HOURS_PRD.GOLD.EARNED_HOURS_JOB_OPERATION`
  - **CDT:** -17.69% (original) → **96.66% row-match, -0.72% aggregate** (after SD-26). Every intermediate stop along the way (SD-19's ~-3.5%, SD-22's -1.49%, SD-24's +2.58%) was built on a formula that was fundamentally wrong (SD-19/23/24, all retracted) — SD-26 is the first version built from the actual documented business rule rather than curve-fit against the reconciliation target
  - **QTS:** **99.80% row-match, -0.0003% aggregate** — essentially exact
  - **CCR:** **95.45% row-match, +0.19% aggregate**
  - **Part-match-rate gap:** dropped from ~11% unmatched to **0.36%** unmatched after the case fix (SD-22) — case variance on `part_num`/`part_revision` was the dominant cause, not placeholder parts or revision drift alone as SD-21 originally characterized. Unaffected by SD-26 (a separate join, not an earned-hours formula question)
  - **Open, unresolved residuals:** the remaining ~3-5% row-level mismatches per company have not been individually characterized yet — likely a long tail of smaller edge cases (reversal timing, data-quality exceptions) rather than one dominant pattern, given how much the aggregate numbers tightened. Not yet investigated further

**Does not exist yet:**
- The semantic view
- ProfitKey ingestion (deferred, SD-01)
- A root-cause characterization of the remaining 3-5% row-level mismatches per company (the aggregate numbers are now tight; the residual row-level noise has not been profiled)

Anyone reading this log mid-project should take Section 6, not Section 5's narrative tone, as the source of truth for "how far along is this."

---

## 7. Forward plan

- [x] Provision workspace + sandbox DB/schemas/resource monitor
- [x] Bronze layer — dbt incremental/merge models over `MATILLION_RAW.CRETEX_EPICOR_KINETIC` (`ERP_LABORDTL`, `ERP_JOBOPER`, `ERP_JOBHEAD`, `ERP_EMPBASIC`, `ERP_JCDEPT`), gated via the enrollment view, QTS+CCR+CDT, 12/12 tests passing
- [x] Silver/intermediate layer — standard-hours recompute, rebuilt on the corrected business rule (SD-26), `int_labor_detail`, built as dbt `dynamic_table` models (SD-04/SD-12)
- [x] Gold/marts layer — conformed `dim_date`/`dim_part` (reused from PRD_DW, SD-18), `dim_employee`/`dim_department` (conformed, local surrogate keys per SD-17), `fct_labor` at the reconciliation target's own grain
- [x] Formal reconciliation suite — `rec_labor_job_operation`, row-for-row comparison; found and retracted a mischaracterized root cause (SD-20), fixed a real case-sensitivity join bug (SD-22), then rebuilt the earned-hours formula from the actual documented business rule after five reverse-engineered iterations proved insufficient (SD-26) — **CDT 96.66%, QTS 99.80%, CCR 95.45% row-match**
- [ ] Profile the remaining 3-5% row-level mismatches per company (aggregate numbers are tight; row-level long tail not yet characterized)
- [ ] Semantic view — Earned Hours, Efficiency %, with declared grain dimensions so weekly/monthly/annual/by-level views are queries, not materialized duplicate tables
- [ ] Final before/after comparison report (object count, manual-effort markers, semantic-layer presence, reconciliation results) for dev-team review

## 8. Explicitly out of scope for this pass

ProfitKey (Anoka/Coon Rapids), the parallel QTS semantic-view build, attendance/utilization metrics, and any production cutover. This is a demonstration, reconciled and documented — not a migration.
